{:edb/db-before-t 573 :edb/db-after-t 574 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533886 50 #inst "2026-10-05T08:20:50.871Z" 13194139533886 true] [17592186078325 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263290 13194139533886 true] [17592186078325 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263297 13194139533886 true] [17592186078325 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263304 13194139533886 true] [17592186078325 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263353 13194139533886 true] [17592186078325 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263367 13194139533886 true] [17592186078326 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263311 13194139533886 true] [17592186078326 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263374 13194139533886 true] [17592186078326 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263381 13194139533886 true] [17592186078326 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263388 13194139533886 true] [17592186078405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263318 13194139533886 true] [17592186078405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263325 13194139533886 true] [17592186078405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263332 13194139533886 true] [17592186078405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263360 13194139533886 true] [17592186078405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263395 13194139533886 true] [17592186078406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263339 13194139533886 true] [17592186078406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263346 13194139533886 true] [17592186078406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263402 13194139533886 true] [17592186078406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186263409 13194139533886 true] [17592186078908 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533886 true] [17592186078908 1145 "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png)\n\n      \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2]$ and  $(2,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $g(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not defined at $x=1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186078910 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186078910 1145 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,1].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[1,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(1,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-1,1).$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and this contains the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263290 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263291 13194139533886 true] [17592186263290 1089 "q-104877" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263290 1100 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-4,1)?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263290 1113 #uuid "1de2feaa-ecdf-5b6a-9c7c-51389b44cfed" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263290 1145 "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-4,1).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1).$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263292 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263293 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263294 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263295 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263296 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263295 13194139533886 true] [17592186263291 1110 #uuid "f2d21171-d578-57ea-82cc-70f7335019a6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263292 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/47dac620347d127e8b10c9678f09918f7ff4ccbc27cc30e224b99af38a52540b.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263292 1111 #uuid "e5e05ade-3560-5bcd-bc76-4a1da47d8fd1" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263293 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/04b4ed554ab43ef25caafe143aa2d4cdf280202f8a3995b10bb19e42f8ec5496.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263293 1111 #uuid "866288ea-10f1-5135-a17e-c9b0f2a12899" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263294 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/deae562b9b3cbbbf07c601c0560084686a2c14453a20dd7148846e2f7d9f623f.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263294 1111 #uuid "6aa4bbc0-6b5a-536d-bcf9-3b14c9205e33" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263295 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263295 1111 #uuid "269d011a-b9b7-5e56-ba7c-3698df605510" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263296 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/cb326fe00fffc0f53f396ecca4975b7ad215d3e05821cfb9b1a365657e2ba97f.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263296 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263296 1111 #uuid "a04f609a-ee17-561e-a7db-d92f6353b22b" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263297 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263298 13194139533886 true] [17592186263297 1089 "q-104881" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263297 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263297 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d6efb9c0bc8d7114c23ad3276303fe6898afcc4e27ad32db354f8e939d6f1c2e.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263297 1113 #uuid "c2bf1f76-1005-59d7-a147-484f36ea43f8" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263297 1145 "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but this is already excluded. \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-3,1)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(-5,0)$ contain the excluded point $x=-3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(1,5)$ and $(0,\\infty )$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=4$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263299 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263300 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263301 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263302 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263303 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263299 13194139533886 true] [17592186263298 1110 #uuid "08483342-8e51-5b2f-b963-30d881265e3e" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263299 1034 "(-3,1)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263299 1111 #uuid "9e49c6e1-4d55-5bee-a8e3-a4d8b895533f" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263300 1034 "(0,\\infty )" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263300 1111 #uuid "485ca6f5-2099-5d35-9faf-4669ec15927a" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263301 1034 "(1,5)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263301 1111 #uuid "9a3fffb7-d329-5f49-9031-290aae4aee3e" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263302 1034 "(-\\infty ,1)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263302 1111 #uuid "57a0a417-e194-5969-ada8-bbb8dea4d316" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263303 1034 "(-5,0)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263303 1111 #uuid "f347298c-527a-5d74-a5b1-15b33fe1b22a" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263305 13194139533886 true] [17592186263304 1089 "q-104882" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263304 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d068feef885b3bb1ca3770a2d2648e9953f3ca9e1823111c507934b39ad71a76.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263304 1113 #uuid "db8e7347-ce26-542d-a0dc-df7db098b3ac" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263304 1145 "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but this is already excluded.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-5,-1)$ \n\nNotice that the intervals $(-3,\\infty ),$  $(-3,5),$  $(-\\infty ,0)$  and $(-3,10)$ contain the excluded point $x=-1,$ so the function is not continuous on any of these  these intervals." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263306 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263307 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263308 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263309 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263310 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263306 13194139533886 true] [17592186263305 1110 #uuid "479fb131-cd6d-55ca-9993-e052da3555a8" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263306 1034 "(-5,-1)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263306 1111 #uuid "e4c1981c-b4c9-5bf6-9d41-54b028b9f1a3" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263307 1034 "(-3,5)" 13194139533886 