"19bb2540-93a4-5094-9d6e-6f7514d17932" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263332 1145 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png)\n\nWe see that $q(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,3],$ and since $(-\\infty ,2]$ lies completely inside $(-\\infty ,3],$ it is also continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-1$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $h(x)$ are not continuous at $x=0$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263334 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263335 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263336 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263337 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263338 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263335 13194139533886 true] [17592186263333 1110 #uuid "99759177-3d20-56ff-9903-5382769d1fc6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263334 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e6f54ad54f5b89f674469c29781d416f0d498855c6d788104d47a8119fa197e8.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263334 1111 #uuid "be3a6032-a8a8-5bdf-8df5-9c94ea3a6300" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263335 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263335 1111 #uuid "9d90d996-5c5f-56b3-b4e5-ba3b0f3df9f7" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263336 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/24551c0a0d905070625c0d1ef76fa942dab6764c1540f7ed317eda533dca06d5.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263336 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263336 1111 #uuid "5495dd0c-bfc0-5f7a-b998-f4c459a9a3f6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263337 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/95587cfce50fd6e9c448bf3485dd98f7dd5bb401778b7a9fac939d356a112677.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263337 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263337 1111 #uuid "37289a07-5ea1-5d3c-8214-4e125cffa24b" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263338 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/99e22a7485ab6e5b2a086418678d90160644814965aac3a3d1aede04e7cc6f2c.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263338 1111 #uuid "ba0de68d-ee83-5dbb-902e-926a8fbb3a98" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263340 13194139533886 true] [17592186263339 1089 "q-105418" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263339 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/b875f560c133e528e4d934ae2722d14c116fac4352f6cca5089f8ec726fe3660.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]$\n- $x\\in [-2,4]$\n- $x\\in [4,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263339 1113 #uuid "b3f87a1e-7af1-56ec-8bb5-254f0576f021" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263339 1145 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,4].$ It is continuous on $(-2,4),$ and also right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=4.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[4,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(4,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263341 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263342 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263343 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263344 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263345 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263345 13194139533886 true] [17592186263340 1110 #uuid "412cb687-fc68-5ada-a756-eca977d828c6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263341 1034 "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263341 1111 #uuid "b610421d-b970-5cbf-8e45-22ab853cdf89" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263342 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263342 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263342 1111 #uuid "ae35c32a-7b7b-5799-af30-11b1a9896635" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263343 1034 "I only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263343 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263343 1111 #uuid "d0633811-d841-5658-8eb3-140465ba26f6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263344 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263344 1111 #uuid "fb8e672e-30fe-52a7-a4a1-722538b115f0" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263345 1034 "II only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263345 1111 #uuid "985118e9-4b7d-5ec2-a131-0eacf3feb74a" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263346 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263347 13194139533886 true] [17592186263346 1089 "q-105419" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263346 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263346 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/c75313fbb97784b27ea0c57068e7221e0f9416ac4857b70723fd7ca949d9aba7.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$\n- $x\\in [-3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263346 1113 #uuid "71a012b2-9b99-5389-ae49-c034c5fda9d6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263346 1145 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and also left-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-3,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-3,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263348 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263349 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263350 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263351 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263352 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263350 13194139533886 true] [17592186263347 1110 #uuid "96d46998-f0fa-5796-b618-0587a303b5f6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263348 1034 "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263348 1111 #uuid "2e660ba2-21f2-5131-8015-7d3497b03d06" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263349 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263349 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263349 1111 #uuid "589f972d-fc94-589a-be29-c7a54c1ac356" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263350 1034 "I only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263350 1111 #uuid "b04caa63-372b-5ee3-a6b9-5fa9426023a4" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263351 1034 "III only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263351 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263351 1111 #uuid "43a55ca8-061a-5c50-a0eb-5164188fc8a1" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263352 1034 "II only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263352 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263352 1111 #uuid "c88f6be2-7a76-5f05-9029-c335410f12b0" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263353 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263354 13194139533886 true] [17592186263353 1089 "q-48234" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263353 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263353 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/43786cb577705d6ae13d44e75203fef441905e9ccfc3713726f07d36c147c7b3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263353 1113 #uuid "8b5e7d86-6590-5293-af9e-465db1c9211b" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263353 1145 "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity. \n\nThe function is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The function is discontinuous at $x=-3$ and at $x=3,$ so we need to exclude these points..\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following: \n\n$\\,$ $(-\\infty ,-3)$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-4,-2)$ and $(-\\infty ,1)$ contain the excluded point $-3,$  so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-2,4)$ and $(2,\\infty )$ contain the excluded point $3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263355 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263356 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263357 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263358 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263359 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263356 13194139533886 true] [17592186263354 1110 #uuid "fbb4ba81-a5d6-5c96-93a4-2eaf749f545c" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263355 1034 "(2,\\infty )" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263355 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263355 1111 #uuid "fa5f3ae4-d5d9-5ff1-bfef-e19423239c5b" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263356 1034 "(-\\infty ,-3)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263356 1111 #uuid "9deb6886-8c97-5952-b3f7-b4bb34366e07" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263357 1034 "(-4,-2)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263357 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263357 1111 #uuid "48452b01-a88b-52cf-9e03-fd0651dc80c6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263358 1034 "(-\\infty ,1)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263358 1111 #uuid "a5f23e81-eb2d-5888-b487-2e61c666507e" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263359 1034 "(-2,4)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263359 1111 #uuid "8dc9349d-be7d-5831-8f88-9899991392d5" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263360 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263361 13194139533886 true] [17592186263360 1089 "q-48348" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263360 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263360 1100 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]?$  You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263360 1113 #uuid "c74056bd-b0fc-5dc2-914f-2bbc6b987726" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263360 1145 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png)\n\nWe see that $h(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,0],$ and since $(-\\infty ,-1]$ lies completely inside $(-\\infty ,0],$ it is also continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-2$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-3$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263362 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263363 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263364 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263365 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263366 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263365 13194139533886 true] [17592186263361 1110 #uuid "5dfd042a-8208-580b-b598-ff2d85c792c8" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263362 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/1326b043af92c5945a5fa679f7daa57eb269ae13edb6d3f9296b3a522678039c.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263362 1111 #uuid "b03424df-5aea-5191-99c1-1cc0fa7a2289" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263363 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/6c04be0224db916731c3f7b4e2093887ffd36a258b19c4112766125ff435704c.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263363 1111 #uuid "ec4858bf-3342-5e79-a865-05e4599f361c" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263364 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/49356dbd9984d40899b321698d641f200d1d28d498323aa88fdfdfac53ed7910.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263364 1111 #uuid "ee4fce77-e994-57c4-9221-5dd27bac5ec4" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263365 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263365 1111 #uuid "59be55c1-cf73-526a-9065-a467e1272bad" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263366 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/da6eb0e1cc28a0bef252e0d66bb232006f6a6e54142b27bb04401ca97025af30.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263366 1111 #uuid "1cde514a-3ff7-5f96-b072-b996c29150c4" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263367 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263368 13194139533886 true] [17592186263367 1089 "q-76110" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263367 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263367 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/760ddc657e8372a3860e2553e4f2b713a8b12544586c708e5f2c1922462567a8.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263367 1113 #uuid "bfd92570-bd08-5565-9c64-935de2ba2369" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263367 1145 "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=3$  but this is already excluded.  \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals. \n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-3,4)$ and $(0,5)$ contain the excluded point $x=3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  $(-\\infty ,0)$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-3$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263369 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263370 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263371 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263372 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263373 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263371 13194139533886 true] [17592186263368 1110 #uuid "2d8ce9b0-6dba-5d74-928a-8627b951dce4" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263369 1034 "(-\\infty ,-2)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263369 1111 #uuid "0ce4394c-2e56-5ebb-8784-e1f49aa67d23" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263370 1034 "(-3,4)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263370 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263370 1111 #uuid "cf03cec3-2928-549e-8e03-c67fe0f6975c" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263371 1034 "(-2,3)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263371 1111 #uuid "6adccafa-f4f3-51de-8168-e95002dddf5c" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263372 1034 "(0,5)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263372 1111 #uuid "60b2a4f1-0ba1-5547-9b51-ba34d405f17a" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263373 1034 "(-\\infty ,0)" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263373 1111 #uuid "f724c74f-9b9d-5f79-b139-2a1dd1299753" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263374 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263375 13194139533886 true] [17592186263374 1089 "q-76124" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263374 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263374 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/04dcf34ee71d47a340cf46334ba08ae2c010bf9c284fc79188ff13ff5d42b243.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263374 1113 #uuid "54f43dcc-abdb-5395-9797-01d9daa06906" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263374 1145 "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-2,1),$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-2,1].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-2,1]$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-2,1]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[2,3]$ and $[2,4]$ contain the point $x=3$ at which the function is not defined, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263376 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263377 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263378 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263379 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263380 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263376 13194139533886 true] [17592186263375 1110 #uuid "4d6485ab-e4a6-57b3-ac2c-020209b5772a" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263376 1034 "[-2,1]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263376 1111 #uuid "26b4a72e-cf61-5d7c-9875-89713655e535" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263377 1034 "[0,2]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263377 1111 #uuid "fb17ef71-f1dc-5542-9d9f-b16910823fc9" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263378 1034 "[2,3]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263378 1111 #uuid "0370ea43-5661-59db-b258-49a1d358b7ed" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263379 1034 "[-2,2]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263379 1111 #uuid "c4a09cba-4049-5743-ba0e-55de2726f6d6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263380 1034 "[2,4]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263380 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263380 1111 #uuid "9667c274-b2ae-537f-8e44-c348998ff678" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263381 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263382 13194139533886 true] [17592186263381 1089 "q-76130" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263381 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263381 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/5d90e9eb29955babde9d3af1ccd690b9d6b8fae6c07ad14da3867318b8487d90.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263381 1113 #uuid "44e5b666-ac66-5dd8-ae44-6b6ed7115689" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263381 1145 "The function is continuous on the open intervals  $(-5,0),$ and  $(0,2\\pi ).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0$ and at $x=-5.$  \n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-5,0)$  and  $[0,2\\pi ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[0,\\pi ]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,2\\pi ]$ and $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ contain the point $x=2\\pi ,$ but the function is not defined at this point, so it can't be continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $[-5,0]$ and $[-2,\\pi ]$  contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263383 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263384 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263385 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263386 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263387 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263384 13194139533886 true] [17592186263382 1110 #uuid "b3df0306-cbbc-59ef-8a6b-81fa5cf9eabf" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263383 1034 "[-5,0]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263383 1111 #uuid "0af0f37f-ecbb-5456-99f5-b08a6639ff67" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263384 1034 "[0,\\pi ]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263384 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263384 1111 #uuid "44214d1a-a80f-5beb-8146-5b532d5e09e7" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263385 1034 "[\\pi ,2\\pi ]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263385 1111 #uuid "8500702a-318c-50a4-a11a-499484196874" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263386 1034 "[0,2\\pi ]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263386 1111 #uuid "b82aa7b8-9a7e-5161-9850-cc5ead98b512" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263387 1034 "[-2,\\pi ]" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533886 true] [17592186263387 1111 #uuid "522882f7-67bb-5666-9bb8-f3442f5d995c" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263388 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263389 13194139533886 true] [17592186263388 1089 "q-76154" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263388 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533886 true] [17592186263388 1100 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2]?$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263388 1113 #uuid "914562d3-5f5a-58ee-b3fa-4cdc5878b28e" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263388 1145 "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png)\n\n  \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2),$ and $(2,6).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2],$ and $(2,6).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $q(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=2,$ so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at some point in the interval $(0,2).$  So it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263390 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263391 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263392 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263393 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263394 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263393 13194139533886 true] [17592186263389 1110 #uuid "69f53737-9fed-57e1-991e-c634e786aa01" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263390 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/2c2038b962892960e2d198038f46c844540dc70f9341487a1fa3cc85e38584db.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263390 1111 #uuid "6f5fb461-0212-52ce-80a1-4c1ca89b5e9a" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263391 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e032276bfe03fe9a8e6df08e361c0a27e556e7901fd3560e5ee649220ba47aee.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263391 1111 #uuid "55d8aebb-159a-5a5f-b20a-d1e075279cb7" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263392 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/6abe338528df6cf89c3738319f4d8be0b87eee3dfbace1667942fc81ebeb5c4a.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263392 1111 #uuid "83e54f39-8d89-5f7f-a87e-6702d0363ef0" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263393 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263393 1111 #uuid "1e0d1b68-398e-5d97-b82e-0b7281edea75" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263394 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/05c57e680f84093ea3740f206db05b239eb50ea0336b86b9c589d93497d90d9c.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263394 1111 #uuid "180267af-9e62-50ff-95b2-5e21665fae69" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263395 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263396 13194139533886 true] [17592186263395 1089 "q-76158" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263395 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263395 1100 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-2,2]?$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263395 1113 #uuid "9b98ca02-7865-58d7-8fbf-1f3bb92e16af" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263395 1145 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/221f7b64094910a5c1037e7071826f27f2ea0472ea93a612e9d38f3c46f5ad27.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $q(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-2,2).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-2,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $g(x)$, $h(x)$, and $p(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $(-2,2).$" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263397 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263398 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263399 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263400 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263401 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263399 13194139533886 true] [17592186263396 1110 #uuid "ff57f0e1-98a8-5eae-8052-d41612468187" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263397 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d146dcbae1741d150191f2848ed6d0b000a913bfc2eb603b459ebe19a6a656aa.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263397 1111 #uuid "f9243421-0a20-54f3-8f74-3e96b0ab3892" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263398 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/2baaf696411a0d3ab6f20bf4977c78f3076f9665f9cac965a37cdca9fb4e8387.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263398 1111 #uuid "eeafc3d3-1f5f-537d-8f10-c3e726c8484e" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263399 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/221f7b64094910a5c1037e7071826f27f2ea0472ea93a612e9d38f3c46f5ad27.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263399 1111 #uuid "9aff30f0-8e1f-5f7c-87d0-18d9dc56c7e9" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263400 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/07b072fd0a49d28fda59c356fe6e8c344d6093f20e23835d26bc386cdd973876.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263400 1111 #uuid "298dc4a0-d057-5a3e-a1c1-b2198bff2755" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263401 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/ddd5e77600b8e9e16f1e985f8a8a306a97e1a9086cde845458e0d06f983c250a.png" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533886 true] [17592186263401 1111 #uuid "7b30b367-ebe1-5390-99ed-dca39ba96ce0" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263402 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263403 13194139533886 true] [17592186263402 1089 "q-76208" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263402 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263402 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/abfd15c8ed134a30ad719f6a857268427511e7f1f15ecfe0d27e5026b5401a83.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3)$\n- $x\\in [3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263402 1113 #uuid "354f4c56-c212-5e79-a571-1e084a5fc978" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263402 1145 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,3).$ It is continuous on $(-2,3)$ and also right continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[3,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263404 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263405 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263406 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263407 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263408 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1041 "selection" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263404 13194139533886 true] [17592186263403 1110 #uuid "83b4ad01-3db6-5023-98ab-19c284784f27" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263404 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263404 1111 #uuid "ee898617-59af-5881-a9f4-c7efe7eae6ea" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263405 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263405 1111 #uuid "bdca861b-9fe0-5d31-9f0c-32734a7d1bf5" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263406 1034 "I only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263406 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263406 1111 #uuid "f81599db-f76f-57e1-83da-75c1e0776f44" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263407 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263407 1111 #uuid "d1e1cc1f-42db-5556-9a5f-9e28200bfbcb" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263408 1034 "II only" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533886 true] [17592186263408 1111 #uuid "7804e45c-a6b6-51cb-8830-4f970fcc2cb6" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263409 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263410 13194139533886 true] [17592186263409 1089 "q-76218" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263409 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533886 true] [17592186263409 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/186ac941080da34b7c149534a2e255852241b28855c0ef15db2beee2d692007a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,5)$\n- $x\\in (4,6)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533886 true] [17592186263409 1113 #uuid "f6acd958-af3c-5c3f-ba74-d4cadbc3229f" 13194139533886 true] [17592186263409 1145 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,3].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,3),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continu